# Rule: SwiftUI Search

Sources

https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/search
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/performing-a-search-operation
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/managing-search-interface-activation
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/view-search
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/environmentvalues/issearching
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/SwiftUI/Adding-a-search-interface-to-your-app

Scope

Applies to search architecture in SwiftUI:

- searchable view modifiers
- search text state
- token-based search
- search suggestions
- search activation state
- result filtering
- search UI placement and ownership

Core Principle

SwiftUI search should be modeled as explicit feature state attached to the view hierarchy that owns the searchable content.

Rules

- Apply `searchable` at the level that owns the searchable content.
- Keep search text in explicit state.
- Keep filtering/query execution separate from row rendering.
- Use search suggestions only when they meaningfully reduce effort.
- Use token-based search only when discrete query facets are valuable.
- Treat search activation as UI state, not business state.
- Keep search results and search-empty states inside the same feature context.
- Never scatter one search flow across unrelated subviews without a clear owner.

Checklist

- Does the correct view own the search query?
- Is searchable attached at the right level?
- Is search execution separated from rendering?
- Are suggestions useful and scoped?
- Are empty/loading/no-results states distinct?
